  "content": {
    "id": "p17-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "10",
    "prompt": "Find the points of intersection of the circles $x^2+y^2=9$ and $(x-2)^2+y^2=9$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(1,2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(1,-2\\sqrt{2})$.",
      "worked": "Subtract the first circle equation from the second to eliminate $y^2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(x-2)^2-x^2&=0\\\\x^2-4x+4-x^2&=0\\\\-4x+4&=0\\\\x&=1\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $x=1$ into $x^2+y^2=9$.\n$$\\begin{align*}1+y^2&=9\\\\y^2&=8\\\\y&=\\pm2\\sqrt{2}\\end{align*}$$\nThe points of intersection are $(1,2\\sqrt{2})$ and $(1,-2\\sqrt{2})$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
